      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The CAN transceivers’ GND1 and GND2 pins are both connected to GND, so the two sides of each transceiver’s isolation barrier share a ground net. The bus-side supply is also labeled 5V_switched_CAN, not a separate isolated supply.",
      "expected": "The CAN interfaces are specified as isolated. Their logic-side and bus-side grounds and supplies should remain galvanically separated, with an isolated supply for the bus side.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "U11: GND1, GND2; both pins connect to GND (sheet cm4-robot.kicad_sch, page 1)."
        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "U10: GND1, GND2; both pins connect to GND (sheet cm4-robot.kicad_sch, page 1)."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN interfaces lose galvanic isolation, allowing ground differences and faults on the bus side to couple into the logic-side ground and undermining the intended isolation protection.",
      "confidence": 0.92
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic connects both ground pins of each CAN isolation transceiver to the same GND net, contrary to the specified isolated CAN interfaces. This defeats galvanic isolation on both buses."
}

